course: dictionary
lesson: 2545
title: Hard words in a safety sign
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A long word can look scary, but the words around it give clues. «A defibrillator gives an electric shock to restart a heart» tells you it is a machine that works on the heart. Read on and you find «public ones, often in a box marked AED», so you know where to look for one.
explain: Ask yourself: what would fit here? A machine is a good guess, and a machine that gives a shock to a heart fits every clue. Then put your meaning into the sentence in place of the hard word and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some words have short forms. A defib is an informal short name for a defibrillator, a bit like telly for television. In a first-aid lesson you may hear both, so you need to know they mean the same thing.
explain: Advice can also hide clues. «Public ones give spoken instructions» means the machine talks you through what to do. When someone collapses, you call 999 in the UK, 911 in the USA, 000 in Australia or 111 in New Zealand.
fact: A defibrillator is a machine that gives an electric shock to the chest.
fact: It is used to make a heart that has stopped beating properly start a normal rhythm again.
fact: «Defib» is an informal short name for a defibrillator.
fact: Public defibrillators, often in a green or yellow box on a wall, can be used by anyone.
fact: A defibrillator does not always work, so you must also call for help and do CPR.
exercise: What is a defibrillator? | A kind of football boot | A bandage for a cut knee | *A machine that gives a shock to restart a heart | A medicine you swallow for a sore chest
exercise: Dan says «defib». What does defib mean? | A phone number for the doctor | A green box that holds sweets | *A short, informal name for a defibrillator | A chair for resting after sport
exercise: Why do public defibrillators give spoken instructions? | *So anyone can use one, even without training | To tell you a joke | To make the machine heavier | To play music while you wait
test: «Unlike in films, the person often needs more CPR and hospital care.» What does this tell you? | It must be a toy | Films are always true about hearts | *A defibrillator alone may not save the person | Defibrillators never fail
test: Which number do you ring in the UK when someone collapses and is not breathing normally? | 111 | 911 | *999 | 000
exam: Why must you still call for help and do CPR even when a defibrillator is nearby? | Because the box is too heavy to carry | Because only doctors are allowed to open the box | Because CPR is a kind of dance | *Because a defibrillator does not always work and the person needs more care
